---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const modes = [
  {
    name: 'Tab-complete',
    what: 'The editor finishes the line you were already writing.',
    check: 'Read what it wrote. It is still your line, and it still carries your name in the blame log.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Console copy/paste',
    what: 'You describe a problem in a chat window and move the answer into your code by hand.',
    check:
      'Check the snippet against the real API and the real file format — not against the model’s recollection of them. Hallucinated function signatures are most convincing when they are plausible.',
  },
  {
    name: 'MCP & tool use',
    what:
      'The model calls tools — reading a file, querying a dataset, running a command — instead of guessing at their contents.',
    check:
      'Prefer read-only tools by default, and let the tool be the source of truth for data. A model that can look it up should never be left to remember it.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Planning mode',
    what: 'The agent proposes what it intends to do, and waits.',
    check:
      'Read the plan before it acts. This is the cheapest checkpoint available: a wrong plan costs a sentence to fix, a wrong implementation costs a review.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Autonomous agent teams',
    what: 'Long-running agents act broadly on their own, coordinating sub-agents, with few checkpoints before they act.',
    check:
      'The most convenient mode and the highest-risk one. Keep it sandboxed and local, scope each sub-agent tightly, and require human approval at the decisions that matter.',
  },
];

const traps = [
  {
    name: 'Context bloat',
    line: 'More context is not more signal.',
    body:
      'Long sessions drift as the window fills. Key details get buried and quality slips, quietly and without an error message. Scope tightly, and reset deliberately.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Over-trust',
    line: 'Fluent is not correct.',
    body:
      'Confident output that is quietly wrong is the characteristic failure of these tools. The real work shifts to verification. Trust, but always check.',
  },
];

const failureModes = [
  'Unit confusion',
  'Precision loss',
  'Subtle physics violations',
  'Visualization misinterpretation',
];

const pillars = [
  {
    name: 'Validation loops',
    body: 'Domain checks at each decision point — units, conservation, precision, provenance.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Human-in-the-loop oversight',
    body: 'Review gates at the decisions that matter. Not everywhere.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Domain-specific safeguards',
    body: 'Physics checks, precision tests, scope-drift containment, tech-stack provenance.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Get started — Scientific Agentic Engineering"
  description="How to work with AI agents on scientific code without giving up scientific rigor: the modes of use, the traps, and the checks that match each."
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Get started</h1>

    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      You do not have to adopt agents wholesale to work with them well. The
      useful question is not <em>which tool</em> — it is
      <strong class="text-gray-900">how much autonomy goes unchecked</strong>,
      and what you have in place to catch it when it goes wrong.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Five common ways of working — and the check each one needs</h2>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      These are five approaches I see often, not a complete taxonomy — you may
      well work in ways that are not on this list. They are also not rungs on a
      ladder, and “further down the list” is not “better.” They complement each
      other, and many practitioners move between them month to month, choosing
      by task. What changes as you go down the list is how much happens without
      you watching — so what has to change with it is the check you put in
      place.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5">
      {modes.map(({ name, what, check }, i) => (
        <div class="border-l-4 border-blue-100 pl-5 py-1">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">
            <span class="text-blue-600 tabular-nums">{i + 1}.</span> {name}
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">{what}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-medium text-gray-900">The check:</span> <Fragment set:html={check} />
          </p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <p class="mt-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      Autonomy trades transparency for throughput. That trade is sometimes worth
      making — but it should be made deliberately, and never on the code whose
      results you intend to publish.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Two traps, whatever mode you are in</h2>

    <div class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
      {traps.map(({ name, line, body }) => (
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-gray-200 p-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">{name}</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm font-medium text-blue-600">{line}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">{body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Why scientific code raises the stakes</h2>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      In most software, a wrong choice costs you aesthetic debt. In scientific
      software, a wrong choice can cost you
      <strong class="text-gray-900">corrupted results that still pass CI</strong>
      — a green test suite over a physically meaningless answer. The failures
      that do this are domain failures, and general-purpose tooling is not
      looking for them:
    </p>

    <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
      {failureModes.map(m => (
        <li class="rounded-full bg-gray-100 px-3 py-1 text-sm text-gray-700">{m}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">What Scientific Agentic Engineering adds</h2>

    <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      General-purpose AI is powerful; scientific work needs domain-specific
      safeguards. The framework organizes those safeguards under three
      principles:
    </p>

    <dl class="mt-5 space-y-4">
      {pillars.map(({ name, body }) => (
        <div>
          <dt class="font-semibold text-gray-900">{name}</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">{body}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <p class="mt-6 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
      The principles are tool-agnostic on purpose — products change faster than
      practices. The
      <a href={`${base}framework/`} class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">framework</a>
      develops them in depth, and the
      <a href={`${base}glossary/`} class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">glossary</a>
      defines the vocabulary used throughout.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-lg bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 px-5 py-4">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-gray-900">A note on institutional policy.</strong>
        Always follow your institution’s current guidance on AI-tool use —
        especially in sensitive roles. Tools and policies are both evolving
        quickly, and this site is not a substitute for either.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
